Creation of View
A View is a virtual table based on the result of a SELECT query. It does not normally store a separate copy of the table data.
CREATE VIEW CSE_STUDENTS AS
SELECT ROLLNO, NAME, MARKS
FROM STUDENT
WHERE DEPARTMENT = 'CSE';
To display the View:
SELECT * FROM CSE_STUDENTS;
Expected Output:
ROLLNO	NAME	MARKS
101	ARUN	85
103	KUMAR	78
105	RAHUL	92
To remove the View:
DROP VIEW CSE_STUDENTS;
Creation of Sequence
A Sequence is a database object that generates a series of unique numeric values. It is useful for generating primary-key values.
CREATE SEQUENCE STUDENT_SEQ
START WITH 106
INCREMENT BY 1
NOCACHE
NOCYCLE;
Generate sequence values:
SELECT STUDENT_SEQ.NEXTVAL FROM DUAL;
SELECT STUDENT_SEQ.NEXTVAL FROM DUAL;
Expected Output:
NEXTVAL
106
107
Use the sequence while inserting a new student:
INSERT INTO STUDENT
VALUES (STUDENT_SEQ.NEXTVAL, 'SNEHA', 'CSE', 89);

COMMIT;

SELECT * FROM STUDENT;
To remove the Sequence:
DROP SEQUENCE STUDENT_SEQ;
Creation of Index
An Index is a database object used to speed up data retrieval. It is especially useful when a column is frequently used in search conditions.
CREATE INDEX IDX_STUDENT_NAME
ON STUDENT(NAME);
Query using the indexed column:
SELECT * FROM STUDENT WHERE NAME = 'ARUN';
The query returns the record for ARUN. The database optimizer may use the index to improve retrieval performance.
To remove the Index:
DROP INDEX IDX_STUDENT_NAME;
SAVEPOINT and ROLLBACK
SAVEPOINT marks a point within a transaction. ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT undoes changes made after that savepoint while keeping earlier changes in the current transaction.
Step 1 – Insert a record:
INSERT INTO STUDENT VALUES (106, 'MOHAN', 'IT', 80);
SAVEPOINT SP1;
Step 2 – Insert another record:
INSERT INTO STUDENT VALUES (107, 'LATHA', 'ECE', 87);

SELECT * FROM STUDENT;
At this point, both MOHAN and LATHA are present in the current transaction.
Step 3 – Roll back to SP1:
ROLLBACK TO SP1;
The insertion of LATHA is undone because it occurred after SP1. The insertion of MOHAN remains.
Step 4 – Make the remaining transaction permanent:
COMMIT;
Expected result after ROLLBACK TO SP1: MOHAN remains, while LATHA is removed.
Difference Between ROLLBACK and ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT
Command	Meaning	Effect
ROLLBACK	Cancels the current transaction	All uncommitted changes are undone
ROLLBACK TO SP1	Rolls back only to a specified savepoint	Changes after SP1 are undone
COMMIT	Makes transaction permanent	Changes are saved permanently

